This PR fixes an issue where window display toggle buttons in the master window don't update their color state when windows are closed using the X button.

## Problem

When a window display button in the master window is ON (green), closing the window using the X button doesn't update the button color to red to indicate the window is now hidden. The button incorrectly remains green even though the window is no longer visible.

![Window button state issue](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f3d7187d-a648-474c-ba91-7266954f5458)

## Root Cause

The delete button click handler in `DebugExtensions.cs` was searching for `Button` elements in window list items, but the actual elements are `Toggle` controls. This caused the color update logic to fail silently.

```csharp
// Before (incorrect)
var button = windowItem.Q<Button>();
if (button != null && button.text == windowName)
{
    button.style.backgroundColor = GetWindowStateColor(root);
}

// After (fixed)
var toggle = windowItem.Q<Toggle>();
if (toggle != null && toggle.text == windowName)
{
    toggle.style.backgroundColor = GetWindowStateColor(root);
}
```

## Solution

Updated the element query to correctly search for `Toggle` elements instead of `Button` elements in the delete button click handler. This ensures that when a window is closed via the X button, the corresponding toggle button color properly updates from green (visible) to red (hidden).

## Testing

The existing test `WindowCloseButton_HidesWindowAndUpdatesToggle` verifies this behavior and should now pass with this fix.

Fixes #61.

